# Target C-II — terminal deficit quantitatively forces a longer FREE face run

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proved candidate awaiting promotion; sharpens the FREE face conclusion when `pi D-q=O(sqrt(D))`  
**Scope:** all-inactive non-anchored FREE reflection ancestry  
**Dependencies:** block-optimized thin/thick upper-block theorem; full-source hull perimeter bound; sharp universal FREE face theorem  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_tooth_reflection_terminal_deficit_face_tradeoff_2026_09_17.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The block-optimized FREE theorem used the thin-edge matching only through a dichotomy:
either that matching is large, giving a superdiameter braid, or it is small, giving a
long lower run.  But the thin matching size is itself paid exactly by the global
terminal deficit

\[
T_D:=\pi D-q.
\]

This gives a continuous source-wide tradeoff.  Near the terminal endpoint `q=pi D`, a
FREE ancestry must contain a substantially longer outer-face run than the unconditional
universal coefficient `0.868655...`.

Put

\[
h_*:=\frac{\sqrt{303}}{52},
\qquad
k_\infty:=\frac1{2h_*}
=1.493660973015707\ldots .
\]

Define, for `t>=0`,

\[
\boxed{
g(t):=
 k_\infty\left(\sqrt{t^2+\frac4{k_\infty}}-t\right).
}
\tag{TD.1}
\]

If along a sequence of FREE ancestries

\[
\limsup_{D\to\infty}\frac{T_D}{\sqrt D}\le t<\infty,
\tag{TD.2}
\]

then one consecutive all-lower run in the original outer contact face has support span

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{span}_N(R)
\ge (g(t)-o(1))\sqrt D.
}
\tag{TD.3}

The function `g` is strictly decreasing.  In particular,

\[
\boxed{
g(0)=\sqrt{\frac2{h_*}}
=2.444308469089536\ldots,}
\tag{TD.4}
\]

so

\[
T_D=o(\sqrt D)
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\operatorname{span}_N(R)
\ge(2.444308469089536\ldots-o(1))\sqrt D.
\tag{TD.5}
\]

At the old block-fork threshold

\[
c_{\rm blk}
=\left(1+\frac{h_*}{2}\right)^{-1/2}
=0.925539574680581\ldots,
\]

one has the exact identity

\[
\boxed{g(2c_{\rm blk})=c_{\rm blk}.}
\tag{TD.6}
\]

Thus the previous statement that a `c_blk sqrt(D)` superbraid forces terminal deficit
at least `2c_blk sqrt(D)` is the endpoint of the full continuous tradeoff.

Combining (TD.3) with the sharp universal FREE-face theorem gives the unconditional
terminal-sensitive bound

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{span}_N(R)
\ge
\left(
\max\{c_{\rm univ},g(T_D/\sqrt D)\}-o(1)
\right)\sqrt D
}
\tag{TD.7}
\]

whenever `T_D=O(sqrt(D))`, where

\[
c_{\rm univ}=0.868655543829230\ldots .
\]

The two coefficients cross at

\[
\boxed{
t_*
=\frac2{c_{\rm univ}}-h_*c_{\rm univ}
=2.011627913893869\ldots .}
\tag{TD.8}
\]

Hence the terminal-deficit theorem improves the universal run throughout

\[
T_D<(2.011627913893869\ldots-o(1))\sqrt D.
\]

For every fixed `Lambda>1`, (TD.3) also gives a vertex-disjoint matching on the same
run of size

\[
\boxed{
\left(
\frac{\Lambda-1}{2\Lambda}g(t)-o(1)
\right)\sqrt D
}
\tag{TD.9}
\]

whose old source lengths satisfy

\[
\boxed{
1<|x-y|
\le
1+\left(\frac{\Lambda h_*}{g(t)}+o(1)\right)D^{-1/2}.
}
\tag{TD.10}

For `Lambda=2` and `T_D=o(sqrt(D))`, this becomes a matching of

\[
(0.611077117272384\ldots-o(1))\sqrt D
\]

with excess at most

\[
(0.273899950493391\ldots+o(1))D^{-1/2}.
\]

---

## 1. The thin matching is paid by `pi D-q`

Use the block theorem's exact thin threshold

\[
\tau_D=D-\sqrt{D^2-1}.
\]

Let

- `m` be the number of thick upper edges;
- `M` be the size of a maximum vertex-disjoint matching among all thin upper edges;
- `R` be the number of maximal lower runs.

The block theorem gives

\[
\boxed{R\le m+M+1.}
\tag{TD.11}
\]

Every selected thin edge has source length strictly greater than
`sqrt(D^2-1)`.  The sharp superdiameter theorem makes its two endpoints exposed vertices
of the full source hull `K`.  These endpoints are fully diameter-inactive, so they are
disjoint from the `q` actual diameter endpoints, each of which is also an exposed full
hull vertex.

Thus `K` has at least `q+2M` vertices.  Consecutive full-hull vertices are distinct
source points and hence at distance at least one.  Therefore

\[
q+2M
\le |\operatorname{Vert}(K)|
\le \operatorname{per}(K)
\le \pi D,
\]

where the last inequality is Cauchy's perimeter-diameter theorem.  Hence the exact
budget is

\[
\boxed{2M\le T_D.}
\tag{TD.12}
\]

Combining (TD.11) and (TD.12),

\[
\boxed{R\le m+\frac{T_D}{2}+1.}
\tag{TD.13}
\]

---

## 2. Thick upper edges supply the numerator

The block theorem already proved

\[
\boxed{
G_{\rm low}
\ge D+k_Dm^2-O(\sqrt D),
}
\tag{TD.14}
\]

where

\[
k_D\longrightarrow k_\infty=\frac1{2h_*}.
\tag{TD.15}
\]

Since every lower support gain is positive, the longest lower run has span at least

\[
S_D
\ge
\frac{D+k_Dm^2-O(\sqrt D)}{m+T_D/2+1}.
\tag{TD.16}
\]

Assume (TD.2).  If `m/sqrt(D)` stays bounded, divide (TD.16) by `sqrt(D)` and minimize
over the subsequential limit `s>=0`:

\[
\liminf\frac{S_D}{\sqrt D}
\ge
\min_{s\ge0}
\frac{1+k_\infty s^2}{s+t/2}.
\tag{TD.17}
\]

If `m/sqrt(D)->infinity`, the right side of (TD.16) is itself
`(k_\infty-o(1))m`, hence is `omega(sqrt(D))`; such a sequence cannot determine the
minimum.

For fixed `t`, differentiation gives the unique minimizer

\[
s_*(t)
=\frac{\sqrt{t^2+4/k_\infty}-t}{2}.
\tag{TD.18}
\]

At the stationary point the quotient in (TD.17) equals `2k_infty s_*`, namely `g(t)`.
This proves (TD.3).

---

## 3. Constants and matching extraction

Equation (TD.4) follows from `g(0)=2sqrt(k_infty)` and
`k_infty=1/(2h_*)`.

For (TD.6), put `c=c_blk`.  Its definition gives

\[
1-c^2=\frac{c^2}{4k_\infty}.
\]

This is equivalent to

\[
\frac{1+k_\infty s^2}{s+c}\ge c
\quad(s\ge0)
\]

with equality at the block minimizer, and therefore `g(2c)=c`.

To solve `g(t)=c_univ`, square the defining equation.  This gives

\[
t=\frac2c-\frac{c}{2k_\infty}
=\frac2c-h_*c,
\]

which is (TD.8).

Finally, a lower run of span `(g(t)-o(1))sqrt(D)` has at least that many edges, while
the lower-edge activation slack on the run sums to at most `h_*+o(1)`.  Markov keeps a
fraction `1-1/Lambda` with individual excess at most

\[
\left(\frac{\Lambda h_*}{g(t)}+o(1)\right)D^{-1/2}.
\]

Parity thinning loses at most another factor two, proving (TD.9)--(TD.10).

---

## 4. Frontier effect

The thin-edge braid is now coupled continuously, rather than dichotomously, to a global
canonical scalar.  A near-maximal terminal population `q=pi D-o(sqrt(D))` cannot merely
avoid the superbraid branch: it forces a FREE outer-face run of support length
`(sqrt(2/h_*)-o(1))sqrt(D)`, almost `2.45 sqrt(D)`.

Together with the universal FREE-face theorem, the reflection obstruction is therefore
controlled at every square-root terminal scale.  The next missing theorem is still
source-preserving: convert one of these long fully inactive planar runs, or several
ordered runs from different FREE modules, into unavailable outer-walk/force-hull
currency or an actual diameter anchor.

C-II remains open.  No theorem-state authority is changed.

**Disposition:** converted the full-hull perimeter budget `pi D-q` into an exact cap on
the thin upper-block matching and solved the resulting minimax problem, producing the
continuous terminal-deficit / FREE-face-run law (TD.1)--(TD.10).
